    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "henrntr",
      "content": "Goa is a multilingual state with a rich linguistic heritage. Konkani is the official language, but Marathi, English, Portuguese, and Hindi are widely spoken."
    },
    {
      "id": "qmzc4iz",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Language</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Script</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Status / Notes</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Konkani</p>",
            "<p>Devanagari (official), Latin, Kannada</p>",
            "<p>Official State Language; 8th Schedule language (1992); mother tongue of majority Goans</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Marathi</p>",
            "<p>Devanagari</p>",
            "<p>Widely spoken; co-official for some purposes; important for competitive exams</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>English</p>",
            "<p>Latin</p>",
            "<p>Language of administration, education, and business</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Portuguese</p>",
            "<p>Latin</p>",
            "<p>Legacy of colonial rule; spoken by older generation and in literature</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hindi</p>",
            "<p>Devanagari</p>",
            "<p>Growing use due to migration and national media</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kannada</p>",
            "<p>Kannada</p>",
            "<p>Spoken in border areas with Karnataka</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "o2ibopl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.2 Religions of Goa"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wtjwb3r",
      "content": "Goa is unique in India for its harmonious blend of Hindu, Christian, and Muslim communities, a legacy of its complex history."
    },
    {
      "id": "tkn4qb1",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Religion</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Population %</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Facts</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hinduism</p>",
            "<p>~65%</p>",
            "<p>Largest group; 350+ temples; Shantadurga, Mangeshi are major temples</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Christianity (Roman Catholic)</p>",
            "<p>~26%</p>",
            "<p>Legacy of Portuguese rule; beautiful churches including UNESCO World Heritage churches</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Islam</p>",
            "<p>~8%</p>",
            "<p>Small but significant community; mosques in Panaji, Ponda, Bicholim</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Others</p>",
            "<p>&lt;1%</p>",
            "<p>Buddhism, Sikhism, Jainism in small numbers</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "td0nuw3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.3 UNESCO World Heritage Sites in Goa"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "CULTURE, ART AND HERITAGE OF GOA",
      "id": "5g6mq16"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4qg76g2",
      "content": "The Churches and Convents of Old Goa are inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site (1986). This is Goa's only UNESCO World Heritage Site."
    },
    {
      "id": "5pgbhjf",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Monument</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Year Built</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Significance</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Basilica of Bom Jesus</p>",
            "<p>1605</p>",
            "<p>Contains the mortal remains of St. Francis Xavier; UNESCO WHS; Baroque architecture</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Se Cathedral (Cathedral of Santa Catarina)</p>",
            "<p>1619</p>",
            "<p>Largest church in Asia; built in Portuguese-Gothic style; Bell of Goa (Golden Bell) is one of the largest bells in Asia</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Church of St. Francis of Assisi</p>",
            "<p>1661</p>",
            "<p>Important for its museum; built on ruins of old chapel</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Church of St. Cajetan</p>",
            "<p>17th Century</p>",
            "<p>Modelled on St. Peter's Basilica, Rome; Corinthian style</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Chapel of St. Catherine</p>",
            "<p>1510</p>",
            "<p>First church built in Goa after Portuguese conquest; originally a temporary structure</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Convent of St. Monica</p>",
            "<p>1627</p>",
            "<p>First convent in India; now museum of Christian art</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Archaeological Museum, Old Goa</p>",
            "<p>N/A</p>",
            "<p>Houses artefacts from Portuguese era and Hindu temples</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "hwhzkqi",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.4 Important Temples of Goa"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2hekcb",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Temple</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Location</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Deity / Significance</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Mahalsa Temple</p>",
            "<p>Mardol, Ponda</p>",
            "<p>Goddess Mahalsa (Mohini form of Vishnu); ancient idol rescued during Inquisition</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Mangeshi Temple</p>",
            "<p>Priol, Ponda</p>",
            "<p>Lord Mangesh (form of Shiva); most visited temple in Goa; white-washed tower (deepastambha)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Shantadurga Temple</p>",
            "<p>Kavlem, Ponda</p>",
            "<p>Goddess Shantadurga; one of Goa's most important temples</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Ramnath Temple</p>",
            "<p>Bandivade, Ponda</p>",
            "<p>Lord Ramnath; rescued idols from Salsete</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Nagesh Temple</p>",
            "<p>Bandode, Ponda</p>",
            "<p>Lord Nagesh; one of the oldest in Goa</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Mahadeva Temple</p>",
            "<p>Tambdi Surla, Bhagwan Mahavir NP</p>",
            "<p>12th century Kadamba-era Hemadpanthi temple; oldest and best-preserved temple in Goa</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Sapta Koteshwar Temple</p>",
            "<p>Narve, Bicholim</p>",
            "<p>Lord Shiva; one of the richest temples of Goa</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Chandreshwar Bhutnath</p>",
            "<p>Paroda Hill, Quepem</p>",
            "<p>Sited on a hill; ancient temple</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shri Mhalsa Temple (Original Site)</p>",
            "<p>Mormugao</p>",
            "<p>Historical origin of the deity now in Mardol</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "zb51pq8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.5 Music, Dance and Performing Arts"
    },
    {
      "id": "4tt8ous",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5.5.1 Traditional Dance Forms"
    },
    {
      "id": "w1njdh7",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Dekhnni — Traditional Goan dance performed by female dancers; semi-classical style",
        "Fugdi — Folk dance performed by women during festivals like Ganesh Chaturthi",
        "Corridinho — Ballroom folk dance of Portuguese origin",
        "Mando — Lyrical romantic song and dance form; sentimental ballad of Goa",
        "Dhalo — Folk dance performed exclusively by women during the month of Paush (winter)",
        "Kunbi Dance — Dance of the Kunbi (original tribal community of Goa)",
        "Shigmo — Spring festival with elaborate folk dances and floats"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "qtd7sxp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5.5.2 Music"
    },
    {
      "id": "nkkszic",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Mando — Most famous musical form; represents blending of Hindu and Christian cultures",
        "Dulpod — Folk songs with lively rhythms, often humorous",
        "Ovi — Songs sung by women while grinding grain; part of oral tradition",
        "Dekhni and Fado (of Portuguese origin) — Goa's musical heritage is unique",
        "Tiatr — Popular Konkani theatre form with songs and dialogue"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dpjqizm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.6 Cuisine of Goa"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "41hpu9g",
      "content": "Goan cuisine is one of the most distinctive in India. It blends indigenous Saraswat Brahmin cooking with Portuguese influences, resulting in unique dishes."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Questions frequently cover Konkani as the official language under the Eighth Schedule of the Constitution.",
        "UNESCO World Heritage Sites in Goa (such as the Churches and Convents of Goa) are common topics in art and culture sections.",
        "Traditional folk dances (e.g., Fugdi, Dekhnni, Dhalo) and important Goan temples are often tested in national and state exams."
      ],
      "id": "9w6hq17"
    },
    {
      "id": "7bh66tj",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Category</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Dishes / Items</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Seafood</p>",
            "<p>Fish curry-rice (most iconic), Prawn balchao, Crab Xec-Xec, Ambot-tik, Caldeirada</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Non-vegetarian</p>",
            "<p>Sorpotel (pork dish), Vindaloo, Chourico (Goan sausage), Xacuti, Cafreal</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Vegetarian (Hindu)</p>",
            "<p>Saraswat food: Foogath, Ghee rice, Sol kadhi, Banana flower curry</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bread</p>",
            "<p>Pao (daily bread), Poi (bread from wheat bran), Kankonn</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sweets</p>",
            "<p>Bebinca (Queen of Goan desserts — 16-layer coconut milk pudding), Dodol, Kulkul, Neureo (Neureos — fried pastry)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Drinks</p>",
            "<p>Feni (State Heritage Drink — Cashew Feni and Coconut Feni), Toddy, Urak</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Spices Used</p>",
            "<p>Coconut, Kokum, Vinegar, Red chilies, Turmeric, Goa's famous spice blend</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fcq16od",
      "content": "Feni has been granted a Geographical Indication (GI) tag and is recognized as Goa's Heritage Drink. Cashew Feni is made from cashew apple juice; Coconut Feni (Mado) is made from coconut toddy."
    },
    {
      "id": "kvg569d",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5.7 Festivals of Goa"
    },
    {
      "id": "fsqsgfs",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Festival</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>When</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Significance</strong></p>"
